<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC
  "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!--
  de.fsembedded.fsupdate1 — D-Bus interface for the F&S Update Framework

  Object path : /de/fsembedded/fsupdate1
  Bus name    : de.fsembedded.fsupdate1 (system bus)
  Provider    : fs-updater-service (root, systemd Type=dbus)
  Clients     : fs-updater CLI (cloud-flow flags), ADU update handler
                (libfus_update_1.so loaded by AducIotAgent)

  Notation    : CLI flags appear here without their leading double dash. XML
                forbids that character pair inside a comment, and this file is
                the interface's published form, so it has to parse with an
                ordinary parser.

  Interface version and stability
  ───────────────────────────────
  Read the `InterfaceVersion` property to learn what this device's bus surface
  offers. It is the version of the INTERFACE; the daemon's package version is
  a different number and moves for reasons that do not touch this file.

  What moves the number, and what does not:

    - Adding a method, signal or property     → InterfaceVersion + 1
    - Widening what an existing member may
      return within its declared type
      (a new enum-like string value)          → InterfaceVersion + 1
    - Fixing a defect so a member does what
      this document already said              → unchanged
    - Wording, comments, formatting           → unchanged
    - Changing the signature or meaning of a
      member callers already depend on        → NOT done here: it needs a new
                                                interface name (the trailing 1)

  The last line is the one with teeth. A signature change is invisible to a
  caller that reads the first fields and ignores the rest, and fatal to a
  generated proxy that checks the whole signature — so a member that has been
  published is never re-typed in place. New information arrives as a new
  member, and a caller that finds InterfaceVersion too low simply does not use
  it.

  Stability of what is here today:

    - FIXED. The session-token protocol, the install and commit methods and
      their meanings, the `InstallCompleted` and `JobFailed` signatures, and
      the property names and types listed below. Callers may depend on these.
    - PROVISIONAL. The free-text `message` in `InstallCompleted` and the
      `reason` in `JobFailed`: human-readable, deliberately improving, and NOT
      to be pattern-matched. The library's refusals are becoming more
      explanatory on purpose, so any match written against today's wording
      breaks silently on an improvement.
    - The machine-readable outcome lives in `InstallOutcome`, added at
      InterfaceVersion 2 and emitted alongside `InstallCompleted` rather than
      replacing it. A caller that must act on *what* failed subscribes to it;
      one that only needs success or failure keeps the signal it has.
    - A method rejection carries the same categories as a named error, added at
      InterfaceVersion 3 and extended at 4 — see "Error names" below. Before that every failure
      arrived as `org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.Failed` with `-EIO`, which is
      still what an unmapped category answers, so nothing a caller relied on
      moved.

  A device whose bus surface predates all of this has no `InterfaceVersion`
  property at all; a failed read of it is itself the answer.

  Error names
  ───────────
  **Switch on the name.** A rejection crosses the bus as a name and a message;
  the handler's errno does not travel with it — a client's errno is derived
  from the name, and an unregistered name yields EIO whatever the handler
  returned. That is why every distinction worth making here has its own name.
  The errno values noted below are what an in-process caller sees; over the bus
  they are not an observable.

  Failures the service itself decides:

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.Busy              (-EBUSY)
        A conflicting operation is already in flight.

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.NoUpdater         (-ENOSYS)
        The updater backend is unavailable.

    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs          (-EINVAL)
        An argument was rejected — a session id that does not match, a value
        out of range, an update type that is not one of the three.

    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.FileNotFound         (-ENOENT)
        The payload path does not exist or is not readable.

  Failures the library reports, named by its own category:

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.Generic           (the library's errno)
        A library failure that carried an errno.

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.NotAllowedState   (-EIO)
        The durable update state forbids the operation.

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.UpdateInProgress  (-EIO)
        Another update is uncommitted.

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.NothingToApply    (-EIO)
        The caller asked to apply and there is nothing pending. Not a failure
        of the update path — the answer "no work to do", which a caller acts on
        differently. It shared Error.Internal until InterfaceVersion 4.

    de.fsembedded.fsupdate1.Error.Internal          (-EIO)
        Any other failure inside the library.

    org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.Failed               (-EIO, -EINVAL, or a read
                                                     error, per site)
        Everything else: a failure outside the library, a malformed message,
        or a category that carries no promise about its cause. Deliberately
        generic — inventing a name for it would suggest a meaning it does not
        have. Do not read its errno as a contract.

  The category names mirror the `kind`
  tokens the install outcome publishes: that is what makes the method door and
  the signal door one vocabulary rather than two. A caller written against an
  older version sees the generic name where it used to, and nothing it relied
  on moved.

  Session token
  ─────────────
  StartDownload returns a uint32 session_id — a monotonically incrementing
  generation counter. Every subsequent method call in the same update cycle
  (ReportDownloadProgress, FinishDownload, StartInstall) passes this token
  back; the service rejects calls whose session_id does not match the active
  session. Signals carry session_id so that a restarted client can correlate
  the signal or property read to the update cycle it initiated.

  session_id = 0 is reserved (never issued). On service start the counter
  begins at 1. It wraps safely; the service only ever has one active session.

  Restart safety
  ──────────────
  A client that reconnects after a crash reads GetState() to obtain the
  current session_id and InstallState. If InstallState is already "finished"
  or "failed" for the session it started, no signal wait is required.
  The standard D-Bus race-free pattern applies: subscribe to signals first,
  then read the property — any signal emitted after subscription is guaranteed
  to arrive; the property read catches the case where the signal fired before
  subscription.

  Download ownership
  ──────────────────
  The ADU handler drives HTTP transfer via the Delivery Optimization SDK
  (ExtensionManager::Download). The service records intent (StartDownload),
  receives progress updates (ReportDownloadProgress), and is told when the
  payload lands on disk (FinishDownload). The service never fetches URLs.

  Install ownership
  ─────────────────
  The CLI cloud-flow flag `install_update` triggers StartInstall. The service
  drives FSUpdate::update_image() through fs-updater-lib. The ADU handler's
  Install() subscribes to InstallCompleted, then reads InstallState to handle
  the case where the signal fired before subscription.

  Apply / Rollback ownership
  ──────────────────────────
  The CLI cloud-flow flags `apply_update` and `rollback_update` call Apply
  and Rollback. These operate on durable lib state (U-Boot variables, RAUC
  slot status) that survives service restarts, so no session_id is needed.
  Apply() returns whether a reboot is mandatory; the ADU handler issues it
  via workflow_request_immediate_reboot().

  Progress sources (InstallProgress)
  ───────────────────────────────────
  Firmware only   : RAUC Progress property, callback-driven via FSUpdate::setInstallProgressCallback()
  Application only: bytes written / applicationImage::application_image_size
                    (size is read from the custom image header)
  Combined fw+app : size-weighted composite of the two sources above

  State enum values (DownloadState, InstallState)
  ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    "idle"        — no operation in progress
    "in_progress" — operation running
    "finished"    — completed successfully
    "failed"      — completed with error
-->
<node name="/de/fsembedded/fsupdate1">

  <interface name="de.fsembedded.fsupdate1">

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Methods
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
      CheckUpdateAvailable

      Caller : CLI `is_update_available`

      Queries the lib state machine to determine whether an update is pending
      (downloaded but not yet committed). Maps to the lib's getUpdateRebootState()
      returning one of the INCOMPLETE_* values.

      Out: available — true if an update is pending and uncommitted
           type      — "fw", "app", or "fw+app"; "" when available=false
           version   — version string from U-Boot env; "" when available=false
           size      — payload size in bytes; 0 when available=false
    -->
    <method name="CheckUpdateAvailable">
      <arg name="available" type="b" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="type"      type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="version"   type="s" direction="out"/>
      <arg name="size"      type="t" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      StartDownload

      Caller : ADU handler's Download() immediately before invoking the DO SDK.

      Registers the incoming download and opens a new update session. The
      service increments the generation counter, resets all phase state to
      idle, and returns the new session_id. If a session is already active
      and not in a terminal state (finished/failed), the call is rejected
      with org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.Failed.

      The service does NOT perform any network I/O. The caller owns the HTTP
      transfer and reports bytes received via ReportDownloadProgress.

      On success, sets DownloadState to "in_progress".

      In:  update_type    — "fw", "app", or "fw+app"
           update_version — version string from the ADU twin manifest
           update_size    — expected payload size in bytes

      Out: session_id — monotonically increasing uint32 (never 0);
                        pass to all subsequent calls in this update cycle
    -->
    <method name="StartDownload">
      <arg name="update_type"    type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="update_version" type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="update_size"    type="t" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="session_id"     type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      ReportDownloadProgress

      Caller : ADU handler, called periodically during the DO SDK transfer loop.

      Updates DownloadProgress (0–100) and emits PropertiesChanged so any
      subscriber sees live progress without polling.

      Rejected if session_id does not match the active session, or if
      DownloadState is not "in_progress".

      In: session_id — value returned by StartDownload
          bytes_done — bytes successfully received so far
    -->
    <method name="ReportDownloadProgress">
      <arg name="session_id" type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="bytes_done" type="t" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      FinishDownload

      Caller : ADU handler after the DO SDK download completes (success or failure).

      On success, the service records payload_path for the subsequent StartInstall
      and transitions DownloadState to "finished". On failure, DownloadState
      transitions to "failed" and payload_path is ignored.

      Rejected if session_id does not match the active session, or if
      DownloadState is not "in_progress".

      In: session_id   — value returned by StartDownload
          success      — true if payload was fully received and hash-verified
          payload_path — absolute path to the downloaded file;
                         empty string when success=false
    -->
    <method name="FinishDownload">
      <arg name="session_id"   type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="success"      type="b" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="payload_path" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      StartInstall

      Caller : CLI `install_update` (replaces the installUpdate marker file).

      The service looks up the payload path recorded by FinishDownload and
      calls FSUpdate::update_image() asynchronously. Returns as soon as the
      install is dispatched; live progress flows via InstallProgress
      PropertiesChanged notifications. Terminal outcome is the InstallCompleted
      signal, which carries session_id for correlation.

      Rejected if session_id does not match the active session, if
      DownloadState is not "finished", or if InstallState is not "idle".

      In:  session_id  — value returned by StartDownload; validates continuity
           update_type — "fw", "app", or "fw+app"; must match StartDownload.
                         Mismatch is rejected to catch integration bugs at the
                         boundary.

      No return value. Clients subscribe to InstallCompleted before calling;
      after the call they read InstallState to handle the race where the signal
      fires before subscription completes.
    -->
    <method name="StartInstall">
      <arg name="session_id"  type="u" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="update_type" type="s" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      InstallLocal

      Caller : CLI `update_install <path>` (unified local install path).

      Mints a fresh session and drives FSUpdate::update_image() against a
      bundle that is already on disk (USB stick, downloader output, etc.).
      Bypasses the StartDownload/FinishDownload half of the session flow.

      Returns the session_id immediately; install runs asynchronously on a
      worker thread. Progress flows via the InstallProgress property and
      PropertiesChanged signals; terminal outcome via InstallCompleted.

      Rejected with:
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.FileNotFound — path missing or unreadable
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.LimitsExceeded — a session is already non-terminal

      In:  path — absolute path to a local F&S update bundle (.fs container)

      Out: session_id — monotonically increasing uint32 (never 0)
    -->
    <method name="InstallLocal">
      <arg name="path"       type="s" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="session_id" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      CancelInstall

      Caller : CLI on SIGINT, ADU on workflow cancel.

      Cooperative cancel. The worker polls m_cancel_requested at phase
      boundaries (between fw and app stages, and before entering RAUC's
      InstallBundle wait loop). When the cancel point is hit, the worker
      pushes a Completion event with success=false, message="cancelled by
      user" — clients see the standard InstallCompleted signal.

      If the worker is currently inside a phase that has no cancel hook
      (e.g. mid-RAUC InstallBundle write), the request is accepted but
      will only take effect at the next phase boundary. This is documented
      as a best-effort cancel; for hard guarantees see the safety rules.

      Rejected with:
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.InvalidArgs — session_id != active session
        org.freedesktop.DBus.Error.FileNotFound — no install in progress

      In: session_id — value returned by InstallLocal / StartDownload
    -->
    <method name="CancelInstall">
      <arg name="session_id" type="u" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Apply

      Caller : CLI `apply_update` cloud-mode (replaces the applyUpdate marker file).

      Apply does NOT commit and does NOT resolve any INCOMPLETE_* state; it
      writes nothing at all. The install already put the target slot at the
      head of the boot order with a full boot budget, so all Apply reports is
      whether the pending state still needs a reboot to take effect. A reboot
      reaching the device by any other cause lands in exactly the same place —
      that equivalence is the invariant, and it is why Apply must stay free of
      durable writes.

      Confirming the update is a separate, later step: see Commit, which the
      decider calls only once the device is running the new slot.

      This method does not reboot. The caller does, when reboot_required is
      true; the ADU handler uses workflow_request_immediate_reboot().

      No session_id: Apply reads durable lib state (U-Boot variables) that is
      independent of the in-memory session.

      Rejected if InstallState is not "finished".

      Out: reboot_required — true for firmware updates; false for app-only
    -->
    <method name="Apply">
      <arg name="reboot_required" type="b" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      Rollback

      Caller : ADU handler Rollback() — CLI `rollback_update` is local-flow only and calls FSUpdate directly.

      Initiates a rollback through FSUpdate. After rollback the service
      resets to idle state. RebootPending may be set if the firmware rollback
      path requires a reboot to finalise.

      No session_id: operates on durable lib state.

      No arguments; no return value. Errors reported via D-Bus error replies.
    -->
    <method name="Rollback">
    </method>

    <!--
      Commit

      Caller : the decider — the application or operator that judges the update
      healthy. Also the cancel flow, for the second role below.

      Commit has two roles, and both are reached through this one method.

      1. The health verdict on a pending update. Under health-by-application
         nothing else renders it: the boot-time confirm deliberately leaves a
         healthy pending update alone, and the autonomous mark-good service
         only restores a boot counter. Call it AFTER the reboot, from the slot
         the update installed. The library reads the reboot from evidence
         rather than from a marker, so a commit attempted before the device is
         running the new slot is refused, not silently accepted.

      2. Finalising a rollback whose reboot has already happened — here the
         device is running the slot it rolled back TO, and the decision was
         made by whoever asked for the rollback. Nothing is being judged.

      Until it is called the update stays pending, and a pending state blocks
      every further install. Leaving it uncalled therefore does not preserve
      the update; it makes the device un-updateable while the boot budget
      erodes toward a fallback.

      Does not replace the CLI's local `commit_update`, which calls the library
      directly: the boot-time confirm runs before this daemon is necessarily
      up, so routing it through D-Bus would add a boot ordering dependency.

      Both paths converge on the same durable state, and what makes them
      converge is named rather than assumed: every write goes through the
      environment library, which takes a blocking lock around it, so the two
      writers serialise whatever order they start in. The unit additionally
      declares After=fus-update-confirm.service — the direction that costs
      nothing — so a caller reaching the bus does not race a confirm still
      deciding. That ordering records the intent; the lock is the guarantee,
      and it is the one to check if this ever appears to fail.

      Out: committed — true if a pending update was confirmed; false if there
      was nothing to confirm.
    -->
    <method name="Commit">
      <arg name="committed" type="b" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
      GetState

      Caller : diagnostics tooling, busctl, restarted clients.

      Returns a snapshot of all service state as a variant dict. A client that
      reconnects after a crash calls this to obtain the current session_id and
      phase states before deciding whether to wait for a signal or act on the
      already-terminal state.

      Keys and value types:
        "SessionId"        u   (0 = no active session)
        "UpdateAvailable"  b
        "UpdateType"       s
        "UpdateVersion"    s
        "UpdateSize"       t
        "DownloadState"    s   ("idle"|"in_progress"|"finished"|"failed")
        "DownloadProgress" i   (0–100)
        "InstallState"     s   ("idle"|"in_progress"|"finished"|"failed")
        "InstallProgress"  i   (0–100)
        "RebootPending"    b

      Out: state — variant dict a{sv}
    -->
    <method name="GetState">
      <arg name="state" type="a{sv}" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Properties (all read-only; changes emitted via PropertiesChanged)
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
      The version of THIS interface, not of the daemon. A caller sees only the
      bus, and the two numbers do not move together: the daemon's package
      version moves for reasons that leave the bus surface untouched.

      Read it before deciding which members to use. Constant while the daemon
      runs, so it may be read once and cached; it does not emit
      PropertiesChanged.

      What moves it, and what does not — see "Interface version and stability"
      below.
    -->
    <property name="InterfaceVersion" type="u" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Current session generation counter. 0 = no active session.
      Matches the session_id returned by the most recent StartDownload.
      Included in GetState() dict for atomic snapshot; also exposed as a
      property for lightweight polling.
    -->
    <property name="SessionId"        type="u" access="read"/>

    <!-- true iff an update slot is queued and awaiting install or commit -->
    <property name="UpdateAvailable"  type="b" access="read"/>

    <!-- "fw", "app", "fw+app", or "" when no update is queued -->
    <property name="UpdateType"       type="s" access="read"/>

    <!-- version string from the ADU twin manifest; "" when no update is queued -->
    <property name="UpdateVersion"    type="s" access="read"/>

    <!-- expected payload size in bytes; 0 when no update is queued -->
    <property name="UpdateSize"       type="t" access="read"/>

    <!-- download phase state: "idle"|"in_progress"|"finished"|"failed" -->
    <property name="DownloadState"    type="s" access="read"/>

    <!-- download completion, 0–100 -->
    <property name="DownloadProgress" type="i" access="read"/>

    <!-- install phase state: "idle"|"in_progress"|"finished"|"failed" -->
    <property name="InstallState"     type="s" access="read"/>

    <!--
      Install completion, 0–100.
      Source depends on update type:
        fw only    — RAUC Progress property from de.pengutronix.rauc
        app only   — bytes written / application_image_size (from image header)
        fw+app     — size-weighted composite of the two above
    -->
    <property name="InstallProgress"  type="i" access="read"/>

    <!--
      true from the point Apply() is called for a firmware update until the
      system reboots. Always false for application-only updates.
    -->
    <property name="RebootPending"    type="b" access="read"/>

    <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
         Signals
         ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

    <!--
      InstallCompleted

      Emitted when FSUpdate::update_image() returns, regardless of outcome.

      The ADU handler's Install() uses this signal as follows:
        1. Subscribe to InstallCompleted.
        2. Read InstallState property.
        3. If InstallState is already "finished" or "failed", use it directly.
        4. Otherwise block on the event loop until the signal arrives.
        5. Match session_id against the value obtained from StartDownload.

      session_id — matches the active session; used to reject stale signals
                   from a previous update cycle after a service restart
      success    — true if update_image() succeeded
      message    — human-readable outcome; empty string on success
    -->
    <signal name="InstallCompleted">
      <arg name="session_id" type="u"/>
      <arg name="success"    type="b"/>
      <arg name="message"    type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!--
      InstallOutcome            (InterfaceVersion 2 and later)

      The same terminal event as InstallCompleted, carrying the cause a program
      can act on. Emitted immediately after it, always, never instead of it — a
      subscriber to the older signal loses nothing, and one that wants the cause
      subscribes to this one instead of parsing a sentence.

      A separate signal rather than three more fields on InstallCompleted: a
      caller that reads the leading arguments would not notice a widened
      signature, while a generated proxy that checks the whole signature would
      reject the message outright. A published member is not re-typed; see
      "Interface version and stability" above.

      session_id — the session this outcome belongs to
      success    — same value as InstallCompleted's
      errorno    — the errno the library carried, or 0 when it carried none.

                   **This is the install path's errno, and it is an open set.**
                   It is whatever the failing operation reported — ENOENT for a
                   payload file that is gone by the time the install starts
                   (nothing revalidates the path between FinishDownload and
                   StartInstall), EPERM for a container holding neither a
                   firmware nor an application member, and any errno an
                   underlying mkdir/open/read produced (EACCES, ENOSPC, EROFS,
                   EINVAL, …).

                   Do NOT map these to the command-line tool's 54 / 56. Those
                   codes belong to the *slot* verbs, where ENOENT means "target
                   slot never provisioned" and EPERM means "target marked bad".
                   The same numbers mean something else here, and translating
                   them across would report a missing bundle as a bad slot.
                   Use `errorno` to tell failures apart and to log, not as a
                   code to hand on unchanged.

      kind       — the library's own failure category, as it names it. The
                   token set is the library's `fs::Error`, which is append-only
                   and ABI-stable, plus two the service adds for outcomes the
                   library never sees:
                     ""                   success
                     "cancelled"          the caller cancelled; errorno is 0
                     "generic"            a library failure that MAY carry an
                                          errno; 0 means none was carried
                     "update_in_progress" another update is uncommitted
                     "not_allowed_state"  the durable state forbids it
                     "internal"           any other library failure
                     "system"             a failure outside the library
                     "unknown"            a non-standard exception was caught
                   The set may grow — a caller must treat an unrecognised token
                   as a plain failure rather than refusing to parse.
    -->
    <signal name="InstallOutcome">
      <arg name="session_id" type="u"/>
      <arg name="success"    type="b"/>
      <arg name="errorno"    type="i"/>
      <arg name="kind"       type="s"/>
    </signal>

    <!--
      JobFailed

      Emitted when an unexpected error interrupts a session before it reaches
      normal completion — for example a lib panic or a RAUC bus disconnect
      mid-install. Not emitted for errors already covered by
      InstallCompleted(success=false).

      Note: a service restart cannot emit this signal — the process is gone.
      Clients that reconnect after a restart must call GetState() to detect a
      lost session: if SessionId matches but InstallState is "idle", treat the
      session as failed.

      Clients treat this as an unrecoverable error for the named session.
      The service resets to idle after emitting this signal.

      session_id — identifies which update cycle failed
      reason     — human-readable description of the failure
    -->
    <signal name="JobFailed">
      <arg name="session_id" type="u"/>
      <arg name="reason"     type="s"/>
    </signal>

  </interface>

</node>
